#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Set up, check, and run the RetailBench simulator this benchmark scores.

    ./simulator/sim setup           from nothing to a store that answers
    ./simulator/sim status          what is here, what is missing, is it running
    ./simulator/sim run  end_today  take one action        (the three: place_order,
    ./simulator/sim view view_inventory                     modify_sku_price, end_today)
    ./simulator/sim checkpoint      where the store last saved itself
    ./simulator/sim stop            shut the store down

    ./simulator/sim restore         the upstream code, back to the pinned commit
    ./simulator/sim fetch           the dataset, at that same commit
    ./simulator/sim python          the interpreter the simulator runs in
    ./simulator/sim serve           the store's REST server, for this run

The store runs as a server (`server.py`) because booting it reads six hundred
megabytes and a run takes thousands of actions: the boot is paid once, and
anyone — the harness, this CLI, a browser — can ask it what it looks like while
it works. `run` and `view` here are thin clients over that API.

Nothing is clever and that is the point: every command says what it is about to
do, does it, and says what changed. `status` is the one to run when it is not
obvious what state the module is in.

Run with the system `python3`. This script imports nothing outside the standard
library on purpose: it is what *builds* the interpreter the simulator needs, so
it cannot be the thing that needs it.
"""

